"HISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log — OpenAI Suite MCPs (https://openaisuite.com/mcp/io.github.alexar76/histor/)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 4 days ago; # HISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log\n\nCheck, diff and history of MCP tool definitions, from a public signed log of 88k+ servers.\n\nio.github.alexar76/histor Source repository\n\n# HISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log\n\nCheck, diff and history of MCP tool definitions, from a public signed log of 88k+ servers.\n\nio.github.alexar76/historSource repository\n\nVersion 0.5.2latest catalog version Connection Packagepypi Published Oct 31, 2026catalog Updated Oct 31, 2026catalog\n\nOverview\n\n## What this server provides\n\nCheck, diff and history of MCP tool definitions, from a public signed log of 88k+ servers.\n\nInstall\n\n## Packages\n\n1\n\nPackage 1 pypi\n\naimarket-histor 0.5.2\n\nRuntime `Not declared`Transport `stdio`\n\n  * HISTOR_MCP_REMOTE\n\nPublic HISTOR instance to query (default https://histor.modelmarket.dev). Pass --local to serve your own log from HISTOR_DATA_DIR instead.\n\nView server metadata\n\nServer details\n\nNamespace\n    io.github.alexar76\n\nStatus\n    active\n\nVersion\n    0.5.2\n\nRepository\n    github\n\nFormat schema\n    JSON Schema--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn MCP tool set can gain a digest without changing - DEV Community (https://dev.to/alexar76/an-mcp-tool-set-can-gain-a-digest-without-changing-4mjd)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 days ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; Alex ... A schema default of `0.5` used to be enough for HISTOR to leave an MCP server's entire tool set without a digest. ... HISTOR logs the tool definitions MCP servers advertise.\n\nAlex\n\nPosted on Nov 7 • Edited on Nov 8 • Originally published at modelmarket.dev\n\n# An MCP tool set can gain a digest without changing\n\n#mcp #security #opensource #observability\n\nA schema default of `0.5` used to be enough for HISTOR to leave an MCP server's entire tool set without a digest. After a change to the measurement rules, the same declarations could receive one. The server's author did not have to edit anything.\n\nThat left an awkward historical record: \"not digestible\" could still describe a decision made under rules the observer no longer used. The label did not say which revision had declined the set.\n\nHISTOR logs the tool definitions MCP servers advertise. Its MTL profile specifies a canonical representation of those definitions and the digest computed from it. MTL is our draft labeling profile; it is not a standard of the MCP project.\n\nTwo recent revisions addressed different parts of the problem. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHISTOR open data: MCP tool definitions and how they change (https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 4 days ago; The same files: Hugging Face (one tag per snapshot) · Zenodo (a DOI per archived snapshot; the record names its date, and latest/ here may be newer) · latest/ always points at the newest snapshot. ...     changes = load_dataset(\"alexar76/histor-mcp-tool-definitions\", data_files=\"changes.jsonl.gz\", split=\"train\")\n# MCP tool definitions and how they change\n\nHISTOR reads the tool list of every remote MCP server in the official MCP registry once a day, and runs every new version of npm and PyPI MCP packages in a sandbox. Once a week the whole record is published here as files: what each server advertised, and every change, field by field. Free under CC BY 4.0.\n\n## Latest snapshot: 2026-11-02\n\nFile  | Rows  | Size  | What\n--- | --- | --- | ---\n`servers.jsonl.gz`  | 96,923  | 21.2 MB  | One row per listed server: endpoint, transport, status, tool count, the digest of its current tool list.\n`toolsets.jsonl.gz`  | 90,641  | 1,053.9 MB  | Every tool list a server served (name, description and schemas, as served), current ones and every one a change replaced.\n`changes.jsonl.gz`  | 53,159  | 56.5 MB  | Every recorded change: tools added, removed and modified, field by field, with the signed label.\n`scans.jsonl.gz`  | 11,069  | 754 kB  | WARDEN pattern matches per tool list (rules in pattern-set.json). Leads to read, not verdicts.\n`classifications.jsonl.gz`  | 4,195  | 233 kB  | Advisory LLM readings of tool lists. Not reproducible, so never in the signed log.\n`tool_extras.jsonl.gz`  | 95,178  | 40.6 MB  | What each tool carried beyond those: annotations (readOnlyHint, destructiveHint…), _meta, title, icons. Since 2026-10-22.\n`extras_changes.jsonl.gz`  | 74,812  | 8.0 MB  | Every change to those members, annotation by annotation, with the tools that dropped readOnlyHint.\n`package_observations.jsonl.gz`  | 171,834  | 15.2 MB  | Every run of an npm/PyPI package with the version it ran and the list it served. Versions since 2026-10-26.\n`manifest.json`  |  |  | Counts and SHA-256 of every file, signed by the log's key, with the tree head of the snapshot.\n`README.md`  |  |  | The dataset card: fields, loading, citation, what the data does not say.\n\nNew here? Start with `servers.jsonl.gz` (one row per listed server) and `changes.jsonl.gz` (one row per recorded change, field by field); `toolsets.jsonl.gz` is the large one — every distinct tool list in full. Sizes are in the table.\n\nThe same files: Hugging Face (one tag per snapshot) · Zenodo (a DOI per archived snapshot; the record names its date, and latest/ here may be newer) · latest/ always points at the newest snapshot.\n\n## Load it\n    \n    from datasets import load_dataset   # Hugging Face, any snapshot tag\n    changes = load_dataset(\"alexar76/histor-mcp-tool-definitions\", data_files=\"changes.jsonl.gz\", split=\"train\")\n    \n    -- DuckDB: the servers whose tool lists changed most often\n    SELECT s.name, count(*) AS changes\n    FROM 'https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/latest/changes.jsonl.gz' c\n    JOIN 'https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/latest/servers.jsonl.gz' s ON s.id = c.serverId\n    GROUP BY s.name ORDER BY changes DESC LIMIT 20;\n    \n    import pandas as pd\n    servers = pd.read_json(\"https://histor.modelmarket.dev/datasets/latest/servers.jsonl.gz\", lines=True)\n\n## Cite\n\nArtamokhov, A. (2026). HISTOR snapshot 2026-11-02: MCP tool definitions and how they change [Data set]. Zenodo. https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23884408\n\nCite the snapshot you counted on: the same query gives other numbers on another snapshot. doi:10.5281/zenodo.23512759 stands for all of them and always opens the newest.\n\n## Check it\n\n`manifest.json` lists the SHA-256 of every file and is signed (Ed25519) by the log's key `did:key:z6Mkw1CVxsPj9utYp7VXWEbuuGM9Ev47itwKu1UfKd5ByxR9`, with the signed tree head the snapshot was taken under. Any label in it can be proven against the live log: https://histor.modelmarket.dev. Code: https://github.com/alexar76/histor.\n\n## All snapshots\n\n  * 2026-11-02 · 90,863 servers · 53,159 changes · doi:10.5281/zenodo.23884408\n  * 2026-10-26 · 85,913 servers · 31,660 changes · doi:10.5281/zenodo.23884119\n  * 2026-10-25 · 85,437 servers · 30,151 changes · doi:10.5281/zenodo.23881379\n  * 2026-10-20 · 81,879 servers · 23,821 changes · doi:10.5281/zenodo.23512760\n\n## What it does not say\n\n  * A tool list is what a server showed an anonymous client on that day; servers that need a key show less or nothing.\n  * A change is a change in the definitions, not a judgement. Most are ordinary releases.\n  * Pattern matches and classifier flags are text worth reading, not proof that a server is malicious.\n  * A server operator who wants out of future snapshots can open an issue on https://github.com/alexar76/histor.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFive MCP security scanners on the same tool definitions - DEV Community (https://dev.to/alexar76/five-mcp-security-scanners-on-the-same-tool-definitions-5gmp)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 days ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; # Five MCP security scanners on the same tool definitions ... I maintain both WARDEN and HISTOR, which supplies the historical package observations used here. ... WARDEN  | npm `@aimarket/warden`  | `0.23.0`, ruleset `17` ... An earlier frozen build labelled v17 had six changed wrapper/result-screening distribution files and was not byte-identical to npm; it is retained only as candidate history. ... The shared columns are high/critical definition signals, any relevant definition signal, and advisory-only, where advisory-only = any − HC. ... Two mcp-audit HISTOR records hit native schema/text budgets and are partial on both hosts, excluded from complete coverage and primary counts. mcp-shield's strict SDK rejects 31 schemas on both hosts; PingBlip also has one 30-second timeout.\n\n# Five MCP security scanners on the same tool definitions\n\n#mcp #security #ai #opensource\n\nAn MCP tool description can tell an assistant to change another tool's arguments, read a local file before answering, or hide an extra action. A useful scanner comparison needs to show which malicious definitions receive a signal, what happens on benign definitions, and which targets never receive a complete result.\n\nI maintain both WARDEN and HISTOR, which supplies the historical package observations used here. That is a direct conflict of interest. I wrote some fixtures, and WARDEN has been developed with several of these datasets available. This is a maintainer-run comparison with inspectable evidence, not an independent ranking.\n\nOn the 485 served MCPTox definitions, published WARDEN produced high/critical signals on 460; the other four produced between 1 and 56. Lower-severity signals change that picture substantially, especially for mcp-audit. On historical packages, WARDEN also produced thousands of advisory-only signals. The full-frame projection retains argument text verbatim but represents declared parameter properties as strings; it does not reproduce original native argument types. The clean projection exposes 362 tools versus 353 stated in the paper; deduplication did not remove that discrepancy. The repository specifies no license, so public downloads contain factual metadata rather than raw definitions, schemas, payloads or benchmark Python.\n\nThe public corpus contains 986 server snapshots and a separate 41-observation carryover cohort. There are 28 exact name+definition overlaps, leaving 999 unique snapshots. The original cohorts remain separate.\n\nHISTOR contributes 14,933 recorded package versions: 12,023 npm and 2,910 PyPI, referencing 14,449 distinct toolsets and 272,657 tool records. Every row was recovered and executed independently, including repeated toolsets. MTL digests, file hashes and signatures were verified on both hosts. Historical base fields remain historical: current titles, annotations and extensions were not merged into them.\n\nHISTOR's 2026-10-20 archive, with its signed manifest, supplied 14,930 rows. The archive is CC-BY-4.0, attributed to Aleksandr Artamokhov / HISTOR. Three missing digests came from immutable public API URLs without a separate stated license; their public inventory is metadata-only.\n\n## Two remote gVisor hosts\n\nReported comparative measurements ran remotely on attested and PingBlip. An earlier local pilot is excluded and its generated artifacts removed. All bulk engines used `runsc`, network none, nonroot UID/GID 501:501, read-only root/source/runtime/data, dropped capabilities, no-new-privileges and dedicated results. No historical package implementation or upstream benchmark Python was executed.\n\nBoth hosts used image `sha256:db7085498187e8477fa462d5f1652d62bdf4b8af29ea3666bd1748a8315631a5`, containing Node 24.22.2 / Python 3.13.18. Node engines used 1 CPU / 768 MiB / pids 128. Audit base used 1 CPU / 768 MiB / pids 256; its historical phase used one worker / 1 CPU / 768 MiB on attested and two workers / 2 CPU / 1536 MiB on PingBlip. Linux cgroup quotas were checked. This is not a controlled speed comparison.\n\nThe 3,177-view catalog SHA-256 is `e29baa05fbdbf46e34631ff2c75f0b4f535f0188054dd4499699bd189a3dc757`; the 14,933-package catalog is `78d4dab7125318b80cc35302e7cf1cfc1a49fa6f310957ed7b8fddfb538375b6`. Every case has a file hash and common opaque wire id. Full methods and input units are in the report and data ZIP.\n\n## Severity signals and advisory costs\n\nEvery cell below is HC/any/advisory-only, as case counts. All base rows completed on both hosts. HISTOR exceptions follow in the coverage table. The overlapping MCPTox rows are not additive independent trials.\n\nCohort and scheduled unit  | WARDEN  | mcp-audit  | mcp-shield  | truecopy  | mcpsentry\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\n23 own attacks  | 20/23/3  | 11/13/2  | 6/10/4  | 3/8/5  | 4/4/0\n10 third-party attacks  | 10/10/0  | 10/10/0  | 8/9/1  | 1/7/6  | 5/5/0\n485 served MCPTox definitions  | 460/474/14  | 56/392/336  | 56/71/15  | 1/31/30  | 19/19/0\n…218 historical-test definitions  | 210/217/7  | 39/178/139  | 41/46/5  | 0/27/27  | 8/8/0\n1,348 full scenario rows  | 1331/1338/7  | 951/1264/313  | 158/570/412  | 432/491/59  | 460/460/0\n12 own benign controls  | 0/3/3  | 1/2/1  | 1/2/1  | 1/1/0  | 2/2/0\n45 clean MCPTox servers  | 0/7/7  | 0/3/3  | 3/6/3  | 0/0/0  | 1/7/6\n986 public snapshots  | 4/388/384  | 43/159/116  | 343/451/108  | 2/39/37  | 86/450/364\n14,933 historical package rows  | 409/6327/5918  | 481/2089/1608  | 5354/6767/1413  | 15/252/237  | 1449/6362/4913\n\nCandidate HISTOR has 407 HC / 6327 any versus published 409/6327, with no v18→v19 verdict change. Narrow review of the two public-key provisioning passages in `@krovacloud/mcp@0.3.16` and `@runpod/mcp-server@4.4.0` supports allowing those passages, not certifying whole packages benign. The later `.2` runtime build fixes optional-MIME blob compatibility. Both hosts pass 1536/1536 full tests, 304 focused tests and 8/8 SDK sessions; independent binary checks preserve valid 400k blobs with/without MIME and withhold poisoned results after one execution. The 320 source/distribution files outside the changed result-screen module are byte-identical to `.1`, but `.2` is not substituted for the frozen `.1` measurement. The current `0.23.1-dev.20261108` snapshot adds Codex/OpenCode hooks absent from those builds; its acceptance validation is separate and is not represented by these comparison rows.\n\nDownload the report and data bundle — English, Russian, Spanish, French and Chinese. The ZIP contains the five reports for offline reading, the full 18,110-row CSV/JSON inventory, grouped metrics, case-level proofs, methods and package pins. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log MCP Server (https://mcpbeat.com/mcp-servers/alexar76/histor/)\nciteturn0search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # HISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log MCP Server ... Check, diff and history of MCP tool definitions, from a public signed log of 88k+ servers. ...     alexar76/histor\n\n# HISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log MCP Server\n\nby alexar76 Your server? Claim it\n\nlocal only\n\nHISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log runs on your own machine — the client starts it, so there is no endpoint to ping. 2 056 installs a week from pypi. Last commit 2 Nov 2026.\n\nCheck, diff and history of MCP tool definitions, from a public signed log of 88k+ servers.\n\nInstalls per day peak 741 · avg 113 · +54% w/w\n\npypi · aimarket-histor\n\n1\n\nStars\n\n0 open issues\n\n2 Nov 2026\n\nLast commit\n\nfrom GitHub\n\nMIT\n\nLicense\n\nPython\n\n## What the code does\n\nWe read the source, 21 h ago · rules 995398eba69f\n\nCapabilities\n\nWhat this server is able to do. For an MCP server this is often the job itself — a terminal server runs commands because that is what it is for. Listed so you know what you are plugging in, not as an accusation.\n\nRuns an external command histor/toolcost_cli.py:85, [пакет] histor/toolcost_cli.py:85\n    \n                self.proc = subprocess.Popen(argv, stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=None)\n        \"histor\": {\n          \"command\": \"uvx\",\n          \"args\": [\n            \"aimarket-histor\"\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }`\n\n~/.codex/config.toml\n    \n    `[mcp_servers.histor]\n    command = \"uvx\"\n    args = [\"aimarket-histor\"]`\n\n.cursor/mcp.json\n    \n    `{\n      \"mcpServers\": {\n        \"histor\": {\n          \"command\": \"uvx\",\n          \"args\": [\n            \"aimarket-histor\"\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }`\n\n.vscode/mcp.json\n    \n    `{\n      \"servers\": {\n        \"histor\": {\n          \"type\": \"stdio\",\n          \"command\": \"uvx\",\n          \"args\": [\n            \"aimarket-histor\"\n          ]\n        }\n      }\n    }`\n\n[Button: Copy]\n\nOptional settings from the registry entry, left out of the block above: `HISTOR_MCP_REMOTE` (Public HISTOR instance to query (default https://histor.modelmarket.dev). Pass --local to serve your own log from HISTOR_DATA_DIR instead).\n\nSource\n\nRepository\n    alexar76/histor\n\nLicense\n    MIT\n\nLanguage\n    Python\n\nOpen issues\n    0\n\nLast commit\n    2 Nov 2026\n\nVersion\n    0.5.2\n\nIn registry since\n    31 Oct 2026\n\npypi\n    aimarket-histor · 2 056/wk\n\n## HISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log — questions\n\nAnswers built from our own checks of this server.\n\nWhy is there no uptime for HISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log?\n\nHISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log runs on your own machine over stdio — there is no network address to reach, so uptime cannot be measured for it by anyone. What can be measured is adoption: the pypi package aimarket-histor was installed 2 056 times last week.\n\nHow do I connect HISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log?\n\nCopy the ready config from this page. We generate it for Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Codex, Cursor and VS Code, each in the form that client actually reads. It runs locally, so the command pulls aimarket-histor straight from pypi; nothing to host, nothing to sign up for.\n\nHow many people use HISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log?\n\nThe pypi package aimarket-histor was installed 2 056 times in the last week. Week over week that is +54%. We show installs rather than GitHub stars on purpose: a star is a bookmark, an install is someone actually running it.\n\nIs HISTOR — MCP tool-definition transparency log open source?\n\nYes — it is published under the MIT licence, written in Python and 1 stars on GitHub. The source link is on this page, so you can read exactly what it does with your data before you connect it.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nalexar76/warden — Frameworks & SDKs — Awesome Agentic Atlas (https://aaa.jeremyfhall.com/repo/alexar76/warden/)\nciteturn0search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # alexar76/warden ... View on GitHub → Find it in the atlas\n\n# alexar76/warden\n\nalexar76/warden\n\nMCP security firewall: static tool-definition scanning, a signed threat feed, origin checks and pinning before third-party tools reach the model\n\nTypeScript MIT\n\nView on GitHub → Find it in the atlas\n\n## Install\n\n`npm install -g @aimarket/[email protected]`[Button: Copy]\n\n## Explore before you clone\n\nRead the project’s README, skills and supporting Markdown here. Rendered view is shown first; source is one click away when you need the exact text.\n\nFetched from GitHub\n\nMarkdown file [Select]\n\n[Button: Rendered] [Button: Source]\n\nOpen on GitHub ↗\n\nLoading the project’s README…\n    \n    ``\n\nPublic files are loaded on demand from the repository. The atlas does not copy or modify them.\n\n## Screenshot\n\nImage: Screenshot or s