/></a>\n      <a href=\"https://warden.modelmarket.dev/\"><img src=\"https://img.shields.io/npm/v/@aimarket/warden.svg\" alt=\"npm @aimarket/warden\" /></a>\n      <img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alexar76/warden/refs/heads/main/docs/badges/tests.svg\" alt=\"96 tests passing\" />\n      <img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alexar76/warden/refs/heads/main/docs/badges/node.svg\" alt=\"node >=20\" />\n      <img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alexar76/warden/refs/heads/main/docs/badges/warden.svg\" alt=\"WARDEN MCP firewall\" />\n      <a href=\"https://github.com/alexar76/warden/blob/main/LICENSE\"><img src=\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alexar76/warden/refs/heads/main/docs/badges/license.svg\" alt=\"License: MIT\" /></a>\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTwo public records of MCP tool definitions, one day, 23,190 servers: where they agree (https://modelmarket.dev/blog/two-public-records-of-mcp-tool-definitions-one-day-23190-servers-where-they-agree/)\nciteturn0search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: 6 days ago; Each hashes a tool definition its own way. ... Some packages put their environment into tool text. `@sunkimkk/pinterest-mcp@1.2.0` describes its download folder as `/tmp/churn-…/Downloads/…` to heldfast and `/home/histor/Downloads/…` to us. `airweave-mcp-search@0.9.62` names its tool after an environment value, so the tool is `search-heldfast-churn-placeholder` for one reader and `search-histor-envkey-…` for the other.\n\n31 Oct 2026 · 6 min read\n\n# Two public records of MCP tool definitions, one day, 23,190 servers: where they agree\n\n#mcp#ai#security#opensource\n\nImage\n\nSeveral projects now keep a public record of what MCP servers tell models about their tools: HISTOR (ours), heldfast, rugsnare, and signed per-tool grades from AgentAvow. Each hashes a tool definition its own way. If two of them read the same server, do they see the same thing, and can one answer in the other's format?\n\nOn 26 October HISTOR published its weekly signed snapshot at 13:49:50Z. heldfast checkpointed its feed at 13:48:36Z the same day. Both are public, so the comparison below can be rerun from the pinned inputs.\n\n## The formats\n\nAll five use SHA-256 over sorted-key JSON, and four over RFC 8785. They differ in which fields count and in how \"missing\" is written:\n\n | name  | title  | description  | inputSchema  | outputSchema  | annotations  | icons\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nHISTOR tool-set digest  | yes  | beside it  | yes  | yes  | yes  | beside it  | beside it\n"
